{
  "dom_id": "step-q90818",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14274151/assets/c8e205799cd303073f215b734f7916ce4d18763792fb884f79d2ddbe4e70bc27.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above? \n\nI. $f(3)=5$\n\nII. $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n\nIII. The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false.there is no arrow from $3\\in X$ to $5\\in Y.$ So, $f(3)\\ne 5.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the diagram, $7\\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $7\\in Y.$ As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=7$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x)\\ne y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
